{"summary":"Accept the two live defects: legacy-save reserve reconstruction and missing alkalinity credit on shrimp TAN production. Reject the rest because they are already fixed in the current code or, for the reserve-maintenance proposal, because it introduces an unvalidated new metabolism/tuning rule rather than a demonstrated corrective change.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-3-694949da","decision":"accept","rationale":"`migrate_v3_to_v4` is currently a no-op, so legacy v3 saves deserialize `animal.reserve_g` to `0.0`. Because reserve now gates clutch hatching and juvenile maturation, populated legacy saves can load into an artificial post-upgrade starvation state. The migration should reconstruct a bounded non-zero reserve from legacy shrimp state instead of cold-zeroing this new hidden dependency."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-c4d41a63","decision":"accept","rationale":"`route_consumed_food` currently adds excreted and respired nitrogen to `ammonia_total_mg_n_total` but never credits `alkalinity_meq_total`, while nitrification later consumes alkalinity for the same TAN. That systematically biases carbonate chemistry acidic during sustained shrimp waste production. Adding the NHx alkalinity credit is the correct coupling."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-e0885878","decision":"reject","rationale":"The code does lack an explicit maintenance drain on `reserve_g`, but this amendment does not identify a validated correction; it proposes an arbitrary new daily bleed and TAN/DIC source with no calibration basis, parameter contract, or test-backed stoichiometric target. That would materially retune long-horizon shrimp behavior rather than fix a specific proven implementation bug."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-769ef011","decision":"reject","rationale":"This defect is already fixed in the current implementation. `route_consumed_food` computes `actual_o2_consumed_mg`, derives `respiration_scale`, scales respired C/N by available oxygen, and retains the unrespired share in `reserve_g` instead of routing impossible respiration products."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-35499590","decision":"reject","rationale":"This ordering bug is already fixed. `step_daily_shrimp` calls `refresh_carbonate_state(state)` immediately after `shrimp_feeding(state)` and before `update_condition(state)`, so NH3 toxicity and downstream shrimp state are evaluated from post-feeding carbonate chemistry, not stale pre-feeding pH."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-61de90b7","decision":"reject","rationale":"The dissolved-pool fallback is no longer present. `fund_live_shrimp_biomass` now only checks whether `reserve_g` can fund the required biomass and returns `false` when it cannot; it does not synthesize tissue from DOC/DIC/DON/TAN. Existing tests also verify that abundant dissolved pools do not fund hatching or maturation when reserve is short."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-eac07306","decision":"reject","rationale":"This defect is already fixed in the current code. `RemoveShrimp` computes the exported reserve fraction against total shrimp population (`adults_count + juveniles_count`), so removing all adults while juveniles remain no longer wipes the entire shared reserve pool. There is a regression test covering this case."},{"amendment_id":"fr-3-96fc0cd7","decision":"reject","rationale":"The loader still preserves the original schema version for reconciliation. `from_value` captures `file_version` before migrations and passes it into `reconcile_stability_tracker`, and `has_unseeded_legacy_stability_tracker` still reseeds schema-v2 saves that serialized the default tracker sentinel. There is also a test covering the fake post-load instability swing case."}]}